  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/07-carp-like-fishes-1-loaches/homaloptera_cf_leonardi_vietnam_en/",
      "html": "<p>Hillstream loach imported from Vietnam, tentatively identified as Homaloptera cf. leonardi. If identical with H. leonardi, it should reach a maximum length of about 6.5 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/07-carp-like-fishes-1-loaches/homaloptera_cf_leonardi_vietnam_en/",
      "html": "<p>The water temperature should be between 22 and 24°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/07-carp-like-fishes-1-loaches/homaloptera_cf_leonardi_vietnam_en/",
      "html": "<p>Generally speaking, hillstream loaches should be kept in an aquarium that imitates a fast running brook. There should be a good current in the tank. One should offer several types of gravel, sand and stone (up to round river pebbles the size of a large man's fist) so that the fish can choose.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/07-carp-like-fishes-1-loaches/homaloptera_cf_leonardi_vietnam_en/",
      "html": "<p>Homaloptera are micropredators and feed on a wide variety of live and frozen food items. Well acclimatized specimens often also accept dried food (best: granulates), but one should not do any feeding experiments in the phase of settling, because uneaten food will pollute the water heavily.</p>"
    }
  ],
